change:
  id: CHG-025
  title: The source-structure port and its language-agnostic conformance suite
  status: accepted
  intent: Make ADR-0005's layered model real and conformance to it decidable, so a frontend for any language can be contributed and checked without reading its adapter.
  audience_level: developer
  relates_to:
    - ADR-0005
    - ARCH-001
    - CON-002
    - PAT-004

deltas:
  - op: ADDED
    capability: mutation-fitness-loop
    requirement: Source structure is one layered model that frontends fill to different depths, and a frontend is supported exactly when it passes the shared conformance suite for the layers it declares.
    scenarios:
      - id: S1
        when: a frontend declares the syntax layer and is run against the shared conformance suite
        then: it is held to the syntax assertions and not to the symbol ones
        and:
          - passing assertions for a layer it does not fill would be a lie, and skipping them silently would be worse, so the declaration is what the suite tests against
      - id: S2
        when: two sources differ only in formatting or comments
        then: the syntax layer reports them identical, and a changed statement is not
        and:
          - this is the property duplicate detection rests on, and it is asserted in both directions so a frontend that reported everything identical could not pass
      - id: S3
        when: a frontend declares the symbol layer and is asked where a declaration lives
        then: it locates the declaration, and an edit outside it is distinguishable from an edit inside it
        and:
          - this is what the declared-locus gate consumes, and no capability may ask which frontend answered
      - id: S4
        when: a frontend cannot produce usable structure for a source
        then: it reports UNPARSEABLE rather than a partial result carrying no declarations
        and:
          - the JavaParser spike returned a partial tree with no types and no methods, so partial success over an empty tree is absence dressed as evidence
      - id: S5
        when: a capability requires a layer a frontend does not fill
        then: the capability reports UNSUPPORTED for that language rather than degrading silently
        and:
          - a frontend supplying structure but not symbols is a first-class outcome that unlocks three of the four capabilities

acceptance:
  - scenario: S1
    test_id: deterministic/SourceStructureConformanceTest.aFrontendMustFillTheLayersItDeclares
    layer: unit
  - scenario: S2
    test_id: deterministic/SourceStructureConformanceTest.formattingOnlyDifferencesAreIdenticalAndRealOnesAreNot
    layer: unit
  - scenario: S3
    test_id: deterministic/SourceStructureConformanceTest.aDeclaredSymbolIsLocatedAndEditsOutsideItAreDistinguishable
    layer: unit
  - scenario: S4
    test_id: deterministic/SourceStructureConformanceTest.unusableStructureIsReportedUnparseableRatherThanPartial
    layer: unit
  - scenario: S5
    test_id: deterministic/SourceStructureConformanceTest.aCapabilityNeedingAnUnfilledLayerIsUnsupported
    layer: unit

tasks:
  - id: T1
    slice: the layered model in domain, dependency-free and parser-agnostic
    status: completed
  - id: T2
    slice: the SourceStructureInspector port in deterministic, returning the model and declaring filled layers
    status: completed
  - id: T3
    slice: the language-agnostic conformance suite, parameterised over a frontend and its fixtures
    status: completed
  - id: T4
    slice: a deliberately wrong in-memory frontend proving each conformance assertion can fail
    status: completed

non_goals:
  - any frontend, including Java
  - the declared-locus gate, structural distance, complexity and convergence
  - the flow layer, which no planned capability needs yet

risks:
  - The suite is written before any real frontend, so it can only assert what the contract requires rather than what a parser happens to do. That is the intent, and the risk is the mirror image: an assertion nobody can satisfy would not be discovered until the first frontend. T4 exists to catch that by making a deliberately wrong frontend fail each assertion, but a suite that is impossible rather than merely strict would pass T4 and fail the Java frontend.
  - Nothing observable changes for a user in this change. That cost is accepted because the suite is what a contributed frontend is judged by, and a contract written after its first implementation describes that implementation.
